holdings="""**Authority for both holdings:** {{AUTHOR}}'s opinion for a unanimous Court, joined by {{OTHER_JOINERS}}. All nine Justices directly join reviewability, provider coverage, the service-interference rule and its application.

**1. The expired orders remain reviewable.** A challenged action remains reviewable when its duration is too short for full litigation before it ends and there is a reasonable expectation that the same challenger will again face it. The thirty-day assistance orders satisfy the duration requirement, and the Government's anticipated further requests involving this provider establish the required recurrence.

The short authorization period prevents ordinary appellate review before expiration, and the Government's stated expectation concerns this provider rather than possible orders against strangers. Both conditions are therefore established on the actual controversy. Expiration does not remove the Court's authority to resolve the recurring statutory dispute.

**2. Necessary technical assistance must preserve the statutory minimum-interference limit.** Section 2518(4) permits a court to require a covered provider's necessary technical assistance only when performed unobtrusively and with a minimum of interference with the services furnished to the intercepted person. The applicant must support the proposed method and its actual service effects; the least disruptive feasible technique still exceeds that authority when it materially disables the primary contracted assistance service instead of producing incidental interruption.

The provider supplies an electronic communications link and falls within the statutory assistance class under section 2510(15). Coverage establishes who may be directed, while section 2518(4) separately limits how assistance is performed. Hartford Underwriters requires both the enacted grant and its conditions to receive effect. Reading minimum interference as whatever disruption the preferred technique requires would consume the restriction whenever technology makes it consequential.

While interception operated, the ordinary information and roadside-assistance functions ceased working. An emergency button or airbag event sent a tone over the investigators' open line instead of contacting the provider's normal emergency response. An unassured signal to investigators is not continued availability of the contracted service. The loss of that availability establishes the relevant interference without requiring an attempted emergency call or a failed rescue.

The district court's finding of no unreasonable burden on the provider concerns a different object from the customer's loss of service. Reimbursement of provider costs and section 2518(5)'s minimization of intercepted conversations likewise cannot substitute for compliance with section 2518(4). New York Telephone's ancillary assistance authority does not override the specific service constraint. Bach permits technically necessary execution within a valid warrant's authorization; it does not enlarge this statutory assistance power. Ordinary incidental interruption remains governed by the enacted minimum-interference standard. The challenged method crosses that limit, so the order cannot stand."""
write('roving_interception','In re Application of the United States for an Order Authorizing the Roving Interception of Oral Communications','Ninth Circuit No. 02-15635; 349 F.3d 1132','2004-01-27','Affirmed, 9–0; invalidation of the expired assistance orders is preserved.',
"""Stone-Zsela expressly accepts the recurrence exception, provider coverage, substantive service limit, the actual operational application and affirmance. All eight Associates directly join those propositions. The Government's applicant showing in Stone's formulation is the ordinary need to support the method and service effects, not a newly invented numerical proof burden. No fallback or separate opinion is required.

The record establishes lost availability of contracted assistance, not an actual missed emergency or failed rescue. A tone on investigators' line is distinguished from the normal provider response without finding what any investigator did in a hypothetical emergency. Protected identity and target details remain protected. Section 2510(15) is the verified provider definition; the lower opinion's inconsistent numbering does not change the rule.

The assistance question, threshold and remedy are completely resolved. General constitutional surveillance questions lie outside this statutory grant under section 8(a). Section 2522/CALEA, takings and due-process theories were not pursued under section 8(b). Those limits are honored by scope, without repetitive public disclaimers. No new order, redesign remedy or prospective blanket injunction is supplied.""",
'Signed unanimous lead after full merits submission. Two holdings, one compact affirmance, no partial joins or separate writings.',ALL,
'Reviewability, provider coverage, minimum-interference limit, actual application and expired-order remedy',holdings,{
'Event':"**In re Application of the United States for an Order Authorizing the Roving Interception of Oral Communications.** Decided January 27, 2004, on merits submission. Review of the Ninth Circuit's judgment, No. 02-15635, 349 F.3d 1132. The question is whether 18 U.S.C. §2518(4) authorizes compelled activation of a vehicle communications system for interception when that use disables its contracted assistance services.\n\nThe thirty-day assistance orders expired during review. The Government anticipates similar requests involving the same provider. The provider's identity and protected investigative details remain sealed.",
'Participation':PART,
'Public Action':'The judgment reversing the challenged assistance order is affirmed.',
'Judgment & Remedy':f"**Affirmed, 9–0. Supporting:** {ALL}.\n\nThe invalidation of the expired assistance orders remains effective. Those orders supply no continuing authority. The statutory rule governs the recurring assistance dispute involving this provider.",
'Opinion Topology':'{{AUTHOR}} delivered the opinion of the unanimous Court, joined by {{OTHER_JOINERS}}. The threshold, statutory holdings and complete disposition have nine direct joins.',
'Holdings':holdings,
'Precedent Treatment':"- **Hartford Underwriters Insurance Co. v. Union Planters Bank:** Applied for reading an enacted authorization with its independent limits.\n- **United States v. New York Telephone Co.:** Distinguished at its ancillary-assistance setting; it does not override section 2518(4)'s express customer-service constraint.\n- **United States v. Bach:** Distinguished as technically necessary execution within valid warrant authority; the authorization and execution conditions remain distinct here.",
'Law After Decision':"Courts may compel covered providers to give necessary technical assistance under section 2518(4), subject to its substantive protection of customer services. Technical necessity and the lack of a more convenient technique cannot authorize material disabling of the primary assistance service. A later application must establish compliance on the actual operational record, including effects on service availability.",
'Separate Writings':'',
'Procedure After Action':"The mandate preserves the appellate invalidation. A later Government request must independently satisfy section 2518(4)'s assistance and service-interference conditions on the technology and facts then presented.",
'Source Notes':"18 U.S.C. §§2510(15), 2518(4), 2518(5); 349 F.3d 1132 (Nov. 18, 2003), Ninth Circuit No. 02-15635. The interference determination concerns the availability of the contracted service, not a finding that an emergency call was attempted. Protected provider and target details remain sealed."
})

holdings="""**Congress may protect covered calls carried by an interstate telephone network.** Congress may prohibit unauthorized interception of covered telephone communications carried through an interstate communications system even when the particular call begins and ends in one State. Direct interference with that instrumentality supplies the constitutional connection; the prosecution must prove the statutory interception and network-related predicates beyond a reasonable doubt, but need not prove an additional interstate endpoint or a substantial individual economic effect.

**Authority:** {{AUTHOR}}'s opinion, joined by {{OTHER_JOINERS}}, has all nine Justices' direct support for the instrumentality rule, its application and the limited disposition.

Lopez preserves Congress's power to protect actual channels and instrumentalities of interstate commerce. Harris applies that power to a direct intrusion upon an identified transportation instrumentality, and Guillen confirms that a confined protective mechanism need not be recast as a substantial-effects case. Here the conduct is interception of communications actually carried by the telephone system. Local endpoints do not sever that direct relationship, and physical damage to the network is unnecessary to its protection.

Holston distinguishes a statutory reference to equipment's past interstate movement from an adequate constitutional connection. Carnes's case rests on the communication's actual use of the network, not the recorder's remote origin. Home Builders supplies additional means for different regulated classes; those routes do not displace the direct-instrumentality ground. The statutory definition, intentional-interception requirement, exceptions and criminal proof remain independently operative. The local-call objection therefore supplies no constitutional basis to dismiss the reviewed wiretap count.

The independent suppression reversal, new-trial direction on the wiretap count and associated sentencing mandate remain effective. The Court's rejection of the commerce objection does not reinstate the reversed conviction."""
write('carnes','United States v. Carnes','Sixth Circuit No. 00-2103; 309 F.3d 950','2004-02-09','Affirmed in the reviewed constitutional respect, 9–0; independent suppression reversal and new-trial mandate preserved.',
"""Stone-Zsela expressly rejects the Commerce Clause objection on the direct-instrumentality ground while preserving the independent reversal of the wiretap conviction and new-trial mandate. All eight Associates directly support that rule and exact component-specific relief. The ordinary criminal burden applies to enacted offense elements; no new jurisdictional element, evidentiary finding or statutory exception is inserted. No standing fallback applies.

The charged live telephone interception uses the operative pre-1997 definitions; later changes concerning stored communications do no work. The intercepted calls' actual network use, rather than recorder provenance or aggregate crime costs, supplies the constitutional connection. The lower court's separate suppression decision and accompanying sentencing consequence retain their existing force. This is not wholesale affirmance of Carnes's conviction or of ungranted appellate issues.

Rancho Viejo is chronologically available for February 9, but its necessary-inclusion route supplies no additional ground for this direct-network application. The January 27 Roving Interception holding, once durably finalized, concerns a distinct compelled-assistance condition and changes no instrumentality premise. The remaining firearm, search and sentencing questions are outside the granted commerce issue under section 8(a); no speculative neighboring surveillance problem becomes a reserved legal question.""",
'Signed unanimous lead after full merits submission; a single constitutional holding and limited affirmance. Compact judgment with an explicit surviving lower-mandate qualification.',ALL,
'Direct-instrumentality rule, statutory-proof distinction, actual application and preserved suppression/new-trial remedy',holdings,{
'Event':"**United States v. Carnes.** Decided February 9, 2004, on merits submission. Review of the Sixth Circuit's judgment, No. 00-2103, 309 F.3d 950. The granted question is whether federal power to protect interstate instrumentalities permits application of 18 U.S.C. §2511 to unauthorized interception of telephone calls whose endpoints are within one State.\n\nRecordings were obtained through a wiretap installed on telephone lines at Carnes's girlfriend's residence. The Court of Appeals rejected the commerce objection but independently reversed the wiretap conviction on suppression grounds and directed a new trial.",
'Participation':PART,
'Public Action':'The judgment is affirmed in the constitutional respect under review.',
'Judgment & Remedy':f"**Affirmed in the reviewed constitutional respect, 9–0. Supporting:** {ALL}.\n\nDismissal of the wiretap count on the Commerce Clause ground is denied. The independent suppression-based reversal of that conviction, new-trial direction and associated sentencing mandate remain in force; this judgment does not reinstate the reversed conviction. Other components retain their existing appellate mandate.",
'Opinion Topology':'{{AUTHOR}} delivered the opinion of the unanimous Court, joined by {{OTHER_JOINERS}}. The instrumentality holding, application and limited disposition have nine direct joins.',
'Holdings':holdings,
'Precedent Treatment':"- **United States v. Lopez:** Applied for protection of actual interstate instrumentalities and identification of a real constitutional connection.\n- **United States v. Harris:** Applied for direct protection of an identified instrumentality even against intrastate conduct.\n- **Pierce County v. Guillen:** Applied for a fitted protective mechanism without requiring a substantial-effects showing for each instance.\n- **United States v. Holston:** Applied to distinguish remote equipment provenance from an actual constitutional mechanism.\n- **National Association of Home Builders v. Babbitt:** Distinguished as identifying additional routes for different regulated classes; those routes are not exclusive of instrumentality protection.",
'Law After Decision':"Federal protection of covered telephone communications extends to local calls actually carried through an interstate telephone system. The enacted interception and network predicates remain subject to the criminal burden. The direct-instrumentality ground defeats the local-endpoints objection while the independent suppression and retrial mandate governs further proceedings in Carnes's case.",
'Separate Writings':'',
'Procedure After Action':"The lower courts must retain the wiretap count against this commerce objection and implement the existing suppression-based reversal, new-trial direction and related mandate. Further proceedings remain governed by the independent rulings already effective below.",
'Source Notes':"18 U.S.C. §§2510 and 2511 as applicable to the charged live interception; U.S. Const., Art. I, §8; 309 F.3d 950 (Oct. 29, 2002), Sixth Circuit No. 00-2103. The constitutional review concerns the telephone-network interception component, and the separate suppression-based reversal retains its force."
})
